  {
    "episode_id": 49122,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49122.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 129,
    "turn_text": "And the reason I did is because we started seeing a trend. And not just in the United States, but other countries as well. The Chinese are very aggressive. about the issue of controlling resources, whether they're natural resources, whether infrastructure, whether production, agriculture, and other countries. Because they've got a huge population, and they don't have the ability to feed themselves. They've got to go outside. So I get that. But seeing this huge influx of acquisition of assets in other countries, we started looking here within our own shores. And you know what we found out? Over the last decade, the purchases by China in the United States has gone up by a factor of 10. Granted, it's not a huge number at this point. I think 380,000 acres throughout the country. But that trajectory that we're on and what we've seen in other countries made me stop and think, we should do something before we wake up in 10, 20 years and think, oh my gosh, we should have done something.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "China has been aggressively acquiring assets in various sectors including natural resources, infrastructure, production, and agriculture abroad.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "China faces domestic challenges in feeding its large population due to limited self-sufficiency in food production.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The rate of Chinese asset acquisitions in the U.S. has increased tenfold over the past decade.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Chinese ownership of land in the U.S. currently totals approximately 380,000 acres.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The current scale of Chinese land purchases in the U.S. is relatively small in absolute terms.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Future accumulation of foreign-owned assets in strategic sectors could threaten national sovereignty or economic independence.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "A pattern observed internationally is likely to repeat itself domestically if left unchecked.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Proactive policy intervention now is more effective than reactive measures later.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Land ownership by foreign entities constitutes a meaningful risk to national security or public interest.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker’s concerns are shared by a broader audience who would support preventive action.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 49122,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49122.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 132,
    "turn_text": "And you know, does that Why is that a big issue? Well, as you know, our relationship with China is an interesting one, the two largest economies in the world. I would not describe China as being our ally. In fact, they're more an adversary. If we allow them to get a foothold in a very important industry, one that I think national security and food security go together. If they have any control on any part of that supply chain, that puts us in a very, very precarious position. If, in fact, some tensions rise and there's some conflict between our countries, they could turn off that spigot of a very important supply of food to our nation, and where do we find ourselves in? So we've got to get a handle on this. We've got to be smart and strategic. And you know what I found out, which is a little concerning. We don't have a good system in place in this country. We do our best to track this, but to stop it. So we're trying to give federal agencies the the tools and the resources they need to be able to do this, and have that conversation with them on how we can accomplish it. So it's taken time, but the good thing is, Brandi, I've seen over the last two and a half years, many other members of Congress, members of the Senate, other states, even the state of Washington, are waking up to the fact that this is a threat.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
