    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49122.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 147,
    "turn_text": "know, they're not wearing it on their forehead. It's a little harder to determine. And one of the things that is really waking up people to this as being a huge issue, certainly the food supply. But there's been instances throughout the country of concerns, members of Communist Chinese Party companies from China purchasing land or wanting to purchase land near, I would say, sensitive infrastructure throughout the country, near military installations and things like that. We just need to keep our eyes wide open here. And some people think, well, is this fair? Is this something that we should be engaged in preventing another country from investing in ours? I would agree that foreign investment that happens, a lot of Canadians own land in the United States, a lot of Europeans. But one thing's different. Those countries are friendly to the United States. China not necessarily. We really have to be careful about it. If you went to China and wanted to buy farmland, What do you think your chances are of buying a hundred acres?",
